@startuml 05-deployment-diagram
' Deployment diagram. This is an honest drawing of a console program: one machine, one
' JVM, one jar, and state that lives only as long as the process does.

title Online Shop — Deployment Diagram

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam node {
  BackgroundColor #F7FAFF
  BorderColor #4A6FA5
}
skinparam artifact {
  BackgroundColor #FDFDFD
  BorderColor #9DB4D1
}
skinparam database {
  BackgroundColor #FFF6E0
  BorderColor #C99A2E
}
skinparam ArrowColor #2C4A73

node "Customer's computer" as machine {

  node "Terminal" as terminal

  node "JVM 17 or newer" as jvm {
    artifact "online-shop-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar" as jar {
      component "cli" as cli
      component "service" as service
      component "repository.memory" as memory
      component "model" as model
    }
    database "Process memory\nLinkedHashMap per repository\nArrayList for the stock log" as heap
  }
}

node "Payment provider\n<<external, simulated>>" as provider {
  component "SimulatedPaymentGateway\nDailyLimitPaymentGateway" as sim
}

terminal <--> cli : stdin / stdout
cli --> service
service --> memory
service --> model
memory --> heap : reads and writes
service --> sim : authorize(orderId, amount)

note right of heap
  Everything the shop knows is held here and
  is gone when the process exits. That is a
  deliberate limit of this phase, not an
  accident: the repository interfaces are the
  seam a database would be attached at, and
  only Main would change.
end note

note bottom of provider
  Drawn as a separate node because user
  stories 10 and 11 model the gateway as an
  actor of its own. In this build it runs in
  the same JVM; the boundary is the
  PaymentGateway interface, not the network.
end note

@enduml
